# Host-daemon version pins — the version of every first-party service prod runs
# that is NOT a container (SERV-130).
#
# Today that is signet alone. The file is named for the category rather than for
# signet because the category is what makes it a separate file; see below.
#
# TRACKED IN GIT for the same reasons versions.env is (SERV-96): a version change
# becomes a reviewable diff, `git log -p versions-host.env` is the rollback index,
# and promote.yml has something it can write with `contents: write` alone.
#
# ── Why this is not just another line in versions.env ────────────────────────────
#
# It looks like one. It cannot be one, and the reason is a safety property rather
# than tidiness.
#
# deploy.yml fires on a push touching `versions.env` and asks scripts/deploy-scope.sh
# which services to pull. That script maps each changed pin onto compose services by
# parsing the raw docker-compose.yml for `${VAR}` inside an `image:`. A pin no image
# interpolates is unmappable, and an unmappable pin makes it REFUSE — which its caller
# correctly reads as "pull the whole stack".
#
# So a SIGNET_VERSION in versions.env would make every signet promote trigger a
# full-stack prod pull and recreate: the exact SERV-109 regression that script exists
# to prevent, arriving through the file it guards. The fix is not to teach it an
# exception. Its entire safety argument is that EVERY failure means pull everything,
# and an exception is silent in the dangerous direction — a pin later typo'd, or one
# whose service is dropped from compose, would then also map to zero services and be
# treated as deliberate instead of refused. "Scoped to nothing" is a deploy that goes
# green having shipped nothing.
#
# A separate file sidesteps all of it. deploy.yml has no path trigger for this file,
# so deploy-scope.sh never sees it; deploy-signet.yml carries the matching trigger.
# promote.yml's commit stays pure either way — one file, nothing else — which is the
# shape deploy-scope.sh recognises and the shape this file avoids being caught by.
#
# Two other things do not carry over from versions.env, and both are load-bearing:
#
#   * There is no registry. Signet's artifact is a GitHub Release asset, not an image,
#     so scripts/verify-tag.sh — which reads manifests — cannot check this value.
#     promote.yml verifies a signet target by asking for the RELEASE instead.
#   * There is no label to compare against. Nothing stamps
#     org.opencontainers.image.version for a host binary, so the delivery ledger's
#     corroboration comes from /healthz and nothing else (SERV-128).
#
# ── Convention ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# FULL PATCH VERSION, and the leading "v". Both differ from versions.env, deliberately.
#
#   major.minor does not work here. versions.env pins `LYCEUM_TAG=1.12` and lets the
#   registry resolve the newest 1.12 patch on a pull — the one float kept on purpose.
#   `gh release download v1.9` has nothing to resolve; it 404s. A host pin names the
#   exact release or it names nothing.
#
#   The "v" stays because this string is a git TAG and is consumed as one:
#   `gh release download {{ signet_version }}` and deploy-signet.yml's
#   `^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$` guard both want it verbatim. That is the same rule
#   versions.env follows — store the literal string the artifact source needs — and
#   here that source is a release rather than a registry.
#
#   Do NOT read this value as a version to COMPARE. Every version signet itself
#   produces is bare: SGNT-38 stripped the prefix from /healthz, from `signet version`
#   and from the Makefile so the delivery ledger could match a daemon's answer against
#   an image label with strict equality. The ansible role derives `signet_version_bare`
#   for every comparison and keeps the tag only for the download. Comparing the two
#   spellings is SERV-130's original bug: it failed v1.9.1's deploy ten retries deep on
#   a daemon that was serving perfectly, then advised rolling back to the binary whose
#   prefix SGNT-38 had just removed.
#
# ── Rolling back below v1.4.1 needs a second edit ────────────────────────────────
#
# The unit binds `127.0.0.1:4010,172.17.0.1:4010`, and anything older than v1.4.1
# (SGNT-17) treats that whole comma string as one address, fails to bind, and burns
# its restart limit in about 25 seconds — taking loopback down with it rather than
# degrading. The ansible role asserts on this rather than trusting the comment, so a
# promote below that floor fails fast with the reason. Pin signet_addr to a single
# host:port first if you genuinely need to go back that far.

SIGNET_VERSION=v1.9.1
